WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.944
ASP.

2
00:00:00.944 --> 00:00:08.576
NET Core model binding maps incoming request values into action parameters, DTOs, view models, and other objects.

3
00:00:08.576 --> 00:00:17.152
It is a powerful convenience because controllers can work with structured data instead of manually reading every request field.

4
00:00:17.152 --> 00:00:19.512
The convenience can also hide risk.

5
00:00:19.512 --> 00:00:28.796
Bound objects may contain default values, missing values, conversion failures, or fields the client should never have been allowed to set.

6
00:00:28.796 --> 00:00:32.809
Model binding is a starting point, not a security guarantee.

7
00:00:32.809 --> 00:00:44.060
DataAnnotations provide attribute-based validation for common rules such as Required, StringLength, Range, RegularExpression, and custom validation attributes.

8
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:51.141
These can be effective for simple request contracts and are easy for reviewers to see on DTOs or view models.

9
00:00:51.141 --> 00:00:56.648
ModelState then represents the binding and validation state of the request model.

10
00:00:56.648 --> 00:01:06.011
In many API configurations, invalid model state can be handled automatically with a structured error response before the action logic runs.

11
00:01:06.011 --> 00:01:11.204
Safe validation errors should be useful without revealing internal details.

12
00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:17.419
A client can be told that a required field is missing, a value is too long, or a format is invalid.

13
00:01:17.419 --> 00:01:24.579
The response should not expose stack traces, database schema details, secrets, or internal object graphs.

14
00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:34.571
Consistent error handling also helps legitimate clients fix requests and helps monitoring distinguish normal invalid input from unusual patterns.

15
00:01:34.571 --> 00:01:39.449
Model binding and validation should not be confused with authorization.

16
00:01:39.449 --> 00:01:45.665
A model may be perfectly valid and still represent an action the user is not allowed to perform.

17
00:01:45.665 --> 00:01:53.218
A user ID, tenant ID, role field, or approval state should not be trusted simply because it was bound successfully.

18
00:01:53.218 --> 00:02:04.233
Use DTOs that expose only client-controlled fields, validate the request shape, then enforce authorization and business rules against trusted server-side state.
